digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56722-z
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) The paper motivates developing and evaluating a cell-type- and time-resolved epigenomic strategy to test whether HD-vulnerable striatal neurons undergo accelerated epigenetic aging or exdifferentiation, including PRC1-linked chromatin changes, developmental gene de-repression, and loss of striatal identity gene regulation.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) The retained and repaired terminal conclusions jointly motivate the paper-level research proposal."];
  "R1" [label="(0,0,0) Because the molecular basis of striatal neuron vulnerability in HD remains unknown, and epigenetic alteration is a hallmark of cellular aging, epigenetic aging is a reasonable candidate avenue to investigate for HD striatal neuron vulnerability, without claiming it is already an established explanation."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) Using FANS coupled to ChIP-seq or CUT&Tag is an appropriate component of a cell-type-resolved epigenomic profiling strategy for investigating HD striatal chromatin changes."];
  "nImplicit1" [label="(0,0,0) If HD-vulnerable striatal neurons undergo accelerated epigenetic aging/exdifferentiation, then this could mechanistically contribute to their selective vulnerability."];
  "nImplicit2" [label="(0,0,0) If bulk tissue shows an epigenetic/transcriptional signature, then a cell-type-resolved and time-resolved assay is required to attribute it to specific vulnerable neurons and disease stages."];
  "nImplicit3" [label="(0,0,0) If accelerated epigenetic aging is suspected, then measuring both active (acetylation) and repressive (Polycomb-linked) marks across cell types and time can test for identity loss (identity gene repression) and developmental gene de-repression."];
  "nImplicit4" [label="(0,0,0) If Polycomb complexes (e.g., PRC1-related marks such as H2AK119ub) are altered, then including PRC1-linked marks enables testing whether PRC1 mechanisms contribute to gene de-repression patterns."];
  "nImplicit5" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given that vulnerable striatal neurons are central to HD and accelerated epigenetic aging could contribute to vulnerability, therefore a focused research direction is to resolve epigenetic aging mechanisms in the vulnerable neurons with appropriate resolution."];
  "nImplicit7" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Given early H3K27ac loss and striatal identity gene downregulation in bulk and the idea that epigenetic erosion causes identity loss (aging), therefore a plausible hypothesis is that HD involves accelerated epigenetic aging (exdifferentiation) in vulnerable striatal neurons."];
  "nImplicit8" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given the lack of cell/time resolution in prior bulk work and the need for such resolution to attribute signatures to vulnerable neurons/stages, therefore the study should use cell-type sorting and temporal epigenomic profiling methods."];
  "nImplicit9" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given suspected involvement of Polycomb/PRC1 regulation, therefore the study should include PRC1-linked chromatin marks (e.g., H2AK119ub) along with H3K27ac/H3K27me3 and other acetylation marks."];
  "nR8_6" [label="(8,8,6) Currently, down-regulated genes in HD mouse striatum are associated with a selective decrease in H3K27ac."];
  "nS10" [label="(10,0,0) Currently, the study investigates HD striatal epigenetic landscape using FANS coupled to ChIP-seq or CUT&Tag."];
  "nS13" [label="(13,0,0) Currently, the study targets H3K27me3, acetylation marks (H3K27ac/H3K9ac/H3K18ac), and H2AK119ub because PRC1 subunits show altered regulation in HD striatal neurons."];
  "nS3" [label="(3,0,0) Currently, striatal dSPN and iSPN neurons are highly vulnerable to the HD mutation."];
  "nS4" [label="(4,0,0) Currently, the molecular basis of striatal neuron vulnerability in HD is unknown."];
  "nS5" [label="(5,0,0) Currently, epigenetic alteration is a key hallmark of cellular aging."];
  "nS6" [label="(6,0,0) If epigenetic information erodes (exdifferentiation), then cells lose cellular identity, which constitutes cellular aging."];
  "nS7" [label="(7,0,0) If exdifferentiation is occurring, then it can be assessed using histone marks (e.g., H3K27ac active; H3K27me3 repressive) and it manifests as developmental gene de-repression and identity gene repression."];
  "nS8" [label="(8,0,0) Currently, bulk HD striatal tissue shows early loss of H3K27ac at striatal super-enhancers, causing downregulation of striatal identity genes."];
  "nS9" [label="(9,0,0) Currently, because prior bulk analyses lacked cell-type and time resolution, it is unclear whether HD-vulnerable neurons show accelerated epigenetic aging, including accelerated de-repression of developmental genes."];
